<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=thermonuclear-weapon">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Weapons and war</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thermonuclear weapon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1952">1952</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as hydrogen bomb, H-bomb, fusion weapon</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A thermonuclear weapon, also called a hydrogen bomb, is a second-generation nuclear weapon that uses nuclear fusion. Its design, the Teller–Ulam configuration, was outlined in 1951, and uses <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/x-ray">X-rays</a> from a fission primary to compress a fusion secondary. The first full-scale test, the Mike shot of Operation Ivy, was carried out by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enewetak">Enewetak</a> Atoll on 1 November 1952 and yielded 10.4 megatons. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, the United Kingdom, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> later tested multi-stage devices, and the weapons dominated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a> arms race.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thermonuclear-weapon.jpg" alt="Thermonuclear weapon" width="330" height="414" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: United States Department of Energy · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Castle_Romeo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1 November 1952</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Enewetak Atoll</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>Edward Teller · Stanisław Ulam · Richard Garwin · <a href="/empire/united-states">United States</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Also arose independently</span>Soviet Union (1955); United Kingdom (1957); China (1966); France (1968)</p><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>The 'George' shot of 9 May 1951 tested the basic concept on a very small scale and released a small fraction of its yield as fusion energy; the first full-scale test was Ivy Mike in 1952.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/nuclear-fission">Nuclear fission<span>1938</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/manhattan-project">Atomic bomb<span>1945</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=thermonuclear-weapon">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
